.TH "CLOSEWATCHER" "3JS" "March 2, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CloseWatcher \- CloseWatcher
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] interface allows a custom UI component with
open and close semantics to respond to device\-specific close actions in
the same way as a built\-in component.
.PP
The \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] interface inherits from
\f[CR]EventTarget\f[R].
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]CloseWatcher()\f[R]
Creates a new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] instance.
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]This interface also inherits methods from its parent,
\f[CI]EventTarget\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CloseWatcher.requestClose()\f[R]
Fires a \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event and if that event is not canceled with
\f[CR]Event.preventDefault()\f[R], proceeds to fire a \f[CR]close\f[R]
event, and then finally deactivates the close watcher as if
\f[CR]destroy()\f[R] was called.
.TP
\f[B]CloseWatcher.close()\f[R]
Immediately fires the \f[CR]close\f[R] event, without firing
\f[CR]cancel\f[R] first, and deactivates the close watcher as if
\f[CR]destroy()\f[R] was called.
.TP
\f[B]CloseWatcher.destroy()\f[R]
Deactivates the close watcher so that it will no longer receive
\f[CR]close\f[R] events.
.SH EVENTS
.TP
\f[B]cancel\f[R]
An event fired before the \f[CR]close\f[R] event, so that
\f[CR]close\f[R] can be prevented from firing.
.TP
\f[B]close\f[R]
An event fired when a close request was received.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Some UI components have \(lqclose behavior\(rq, meaning that the
component appears, and the user can close it when they are finished with
it.
For example: sidebars, popups, dialogs, or notifications.
.PP
Users generally expect to be able to use a particular mechanism to close
these elements, and the mechanism tends to be device\-specific.
For example, on a device with a keyboard it might be the Esc key, but
Android might use the back button.
For built\-in components, such as popover or \f[CR]<dialog>\f[R]
elements, the browser takes care of these differences, closing the
element when the user performs the close action appropriate for the
device.
However, when a web developer implements their own closable UI component
(for example, a sidebar), it is hard to implement this kind of
device\-specific close behavior.
.PP
The \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] interface solves this problem by delivering
a \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event, followed by a \f[CR]close\f[R] event, when
the user executes the device\-specific close action.
Web applications can use the \f[CR]onclose\f[R] handler to close the UI
element in response to the device\-specific event.
They can also trigger these same events in response to the UI
element\(cqs normal closing mechanism, and then implement common
\f[CR]close\f[R] event handling for both the application\- and
device\-specific close action.
Once the \f[CR]onclose\f[R] event handler completes the
\f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] is destroyed and the events will no longer be
fired.
.PP
In some applications the UI element may only be allowed to close when it
is in a particular state; for example, when some needed information is
populated.
To address these cases, applications can prevent the \f[CR]close\f[R]
event from being emitted by implementing a handler for the
\f[CR]cancel\f[R] event that calls \f[CR]Event.preventDefault()\f[R] if
the UI element is not ready to close.
.PP
You can create \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] instances without user
activation, and this can be useful to implement cases like session
inactivity timeout dialogs.
However, if you create more than one \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] without
user activation, then the watchers will be grouped, so a single close
request will close them both.
In addition, the first close watcher does not necessarily have to be a
\f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] object: it could be a modal dialog element, or a
popover generated by an element with the popover attribute
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Processing close requests
In this example, you have your own UI component (a picker) and you want
to support both, the platform\(cqs default close method (e.g., the Esc
key) and your custom close method (a close button).
.PP
You create a \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] to handle all \f[CR]close\f[R]
events.
.PP
The \f[CR]onclick\f[R] handler of your UI component can call
\f[CR]requestClose\f[R] to request a close and to route your close
request through the same \f[CR]onclose\f[R] handler the platform close
method uses.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] watcher = \f[B]new\f[R] CloseWatcher();
\f[B]const\f[R] picker = setUpAndShowPickerDOMElement();
\f[B]let\f[R] chosenValue = \f[B]null\f[R];

watcher.onclose = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  chosenValue = picker.querySelector(\(dqinput\(dq).value;
  picker.remove();
};

picker.querySelector(\(dq.close\-button\(dq).onclick = () \f[B]=>\f[R] watcher.requestClose();
.EE
.SS Closing a sidebar using a platform close request
In this example we have a sidebar component that is displayed when an
\(lqOpen\(rq button is selected, and hidden using either a \(lqClose\(rq
button or platform\-native mechanisms.
To make it more interesting, this is a live example!
.PP
Note also that the example is a little contrived, because normally we
would use a toggle button to change a sidebar state.
We could certainly do that, but using separate \(lqOpen\(rq and
\(lqClose\(rq buttons makes it easier to demonstrate the feature.
.SS HTML
The HTML defines \(lqOpen\(rq and \(lqClose\(rq \f[CR]<button>\f[R]
elements, along with \f[CR]<div>\f[R] elements for the main content and
the sidebar.
CSS is used to animate the display of the sidebar element when the
\f[CR]open\f[R] class is added or removed from the sidebar and content
elements (this CSS is hidden because it is not relevant to the example).
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqsidebar\-open\(dq type=\(dqbutton\(dq>Open</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqsidebar\-close\(dq type=\(dqbutton\(dq>Close</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqsidebar\(dq>Sidebar</\f[B]div\f[R]>
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqmain\-content\(dq>Main content</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The code first gets variables for the buttons and \f[CR]<div>\f[R]
elements defined in the HTML.
It also defines a function \f[CR]closeSidebar()\f[R] that is called when
the sidebar is closed, to remove the \f[CR]open\f[R] class from the
\f[CR]<div>\f[R] elements, and adds a \f[CR]click\f[R] event listener
that calls the \f[CR]openSidebar()\f[R] method when the \(lqOpen\(rq
button is clicked.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] sidebar = document.querySelector(\(dq.sidebar\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] mainContent = document.querySelector(\(dq.main\-content\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] sidebarOpen = document.getElementById(\(dqsidebar\-open\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] sidebarClose = document.getElementById(\(dqsidebar\-close\(dq);

\f[B]function\f[R] closeSidebar() {
  sidebar.classList.remove(\(dqopen\(dq);
  mainContent.classList.remove(\(dqopen\(dq);
}

sidebarOpen.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, openSidebar);
.EE
.PP
The implementation of \f[CR]openSidebar()\f[R] is given below.
The method first checks if the sidebar is already open, and if not, adds
the \f[CR]open\f[R] class to the elements so that the sidebar is
displayed.
.PP
We then create a new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] and add a listener that
will call \f[CR]close()\f[R] on it if the \(lqClose\(rq button is
clicked.
This ensures that the \f[CR]close\f[R] event is called when either
platform native close methods or the \(lqClose\(rq button are used.
The implementation of the \f[CR]onclose()\f[R] event handler simply
closes the sidebar, and the \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] is then destroyed
automatically.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] openSidebar() {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (!sidebar.classList.contains(\(dqopen\(dq)) {
    sidebar.classList.add(\(dqopen\(dq);
    mainContent.classList.add(\(dqopen\(dq);

    \f[I]// Add new CloseWatcher\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] watcher = \f[B]new\f[R] CloseWatcher();

    sidebarClose.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] watcher.close());

    \f[I]// Handle close event, invoked by platform mechanisms or \(dqClose\(dq button\f[R]
    watcher.onclose = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
      closeSidebar();
    };
  }
}
.EE
.PP
Note that we chose to call \f[CR]close()\f[R] on the watcher instead of
\f[CR]CloseWatcher.requestClose()\f[R] because we don\(cqt need the
\f[CR]cancel\f[R] event to be emitted (we would use
\f[CR]requestClose()\f[R] and the \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event handler if
there was a reason to ever prevent the sidebar from closing
prematurely).
.SS Result
Select the \(lqOpen\(rq button to open the sidebar.
You should be able to close the sidebar using the \(lqClose\(rq button
or the usual platform method, such as the Esc key on Windows.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]close\f[R] event on \f[CR]HTMLDialogElement\f[R]
